course: dictionary
lesson: 8016
title: Reference: mention it, look it up, say where it came from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reference is a word with more than one meaning, so the sentence around it tells you which one is meant. «She made a reference to the trip.» Here reference means mentioning something. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: Reference can also mean a book or website where you look up facts, like a dictionary or an encyclopedia. «The reference section of the library has atlases and encyclopedias.» Here reference means the place to find facts, not a letter and not a note.
explain: When you use facts from a book or website, you give a reference: a note that says where you found the information. A reference can also be a letter from someone who knows you, saying you are good for a job or school. «A reference is a short message from a past employer or teacher saying the person was reliable.»
fact: Reference means mentioning or talking about something.
fact: A reference book is one you look things up in, like a dictionary or an atlas.
fact: «For reference» means to keep something so you can look it up later, and «with reference to» means about or concerning.
fact: On British maps, a four-figure grid reference gives the eastings (along the bottom) first, then the northings (up the side). Remember it as «along the corridor, then up the stairs».
fact: Check the references of a website before you trust it, because anyone can edit some websites.
exercise: «She made a reference to the trip.» What does reference mean here? | *Mentioning or talking about the trip | A letter saying she is good for a job | A book where you look up facts
exercise: Mia writes a project about volcanoes and lists the book she used at the bottom of her page. Why? | *To give a reference, so readers know where the facts came from | To show her teacher she is tired | To make the page look longer
exercise: On a British map, which comes first in a four-figure grid reference? | The contour lines | The northings, up the side | The key, which explains the symbols | *The eastings, along the bottom
test: «Use Wikipedia as a start and check its references, because anyone can edit it.» What are the references in this sentence? | *The notes showing where the information came from | A short letter from a teacher | A map showing the way to the library
test: Which phrase means about or concerning? | For reference | *With reference to | A reference book
exam: Mr Patel writes, «Sam is reliable and hardworking.» What kind of reference is this? | *A letter from someone who knows Sam, saying he is good for a job or school | A grid reference on a map | A note that says where facts came from | A book full of facts to look things up in
